.TH CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_CREATE_WITH_MPROCS 3 "2026-07-12" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_event_loop_create_with_mprocs \- create a persistent event loop
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_event_loop ccol_event_loop_create_with_mprocs(size_t max_events_per_wait,
                        size_t num_lock_stripes, size_t num_reactor_threads,
                        ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mmgmt_procs, char **err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function creates a persistent kernel event queue, an epoll(7)
instance on Linux and a kqueue(2) on FreeBSD. It then starts
the thread or the threads that drive it at once. ccol_select() creates
a fresh epoll instance on every call, and tears it down after exactly
one selectable becomes ready. A ccol_event_loop is different. Its
epoll instance and its threads live for as long as the handle does.
ccol_event_loop_add, ccol_event_loop_modify and
ccol_event_loop_remove change the registrations against it one step at
a time.

Exactly ONE dedicated thread ever calls epoll_wait, for every value of
num_reactor_threads. There is never more than one, whatever the size
of num_reactor_threads. With num_reactor_threads == 1, that one thread
polls and also runs every callback inline. A larger value starts that
same one poller thread, plus (num_reactor_threads - 1) separate
dispatch worker threads that run the callbacks. The total number of OS
threads for a given num_reactor_threads is therefore always exactly
that value, in both shapes. Every thread is ready to work as soon as
this call returns. This mirrors the "ready to work the moment that you
get the handle" ergonomics of ccol_create_cthread_pool().

With num_reactor_threads > 1, two properties of correctness hold. A
single thread gets both of them free, because it has only one caller.
First, the callbacks of one registration never run at the same time as
themselves. Second, a read registration and a write registration that
share the same fd never run at the same time as each other. That
second rule is stricter than "no self-concurrency" alone. A pair of
callbacks that shares state across both directions of one fd therefore
needs no lock of its own. One TLS connection object is such a shared
state.

The loop also never collects a second dispatch for the same
registration while an earlier one still waits in a queue or runs.
Application code can therefore call ccol_event_loop_modify from inside
an in-flight callback. That is a supported pattern, and a common one.
It never races a second dispatch for that same registration that the
loop collects at the same time. See ccol_event_loop_reg_generation(3)
for the separate identity token that this design also makes available
to a caller.

The library builds a registration from the same ccol_selectable type
that ccol_select uses. The builders are ccol_selectable_from_fd,
ccol_selectable_from_circq, ccol_selectable_from_dynq and
ccol_selectable_from_chan. Like ccol_select, ccol_event_loop never
does the receive or the send itself, for any type of selectable. The
callback always does it explicitly. See ccol_event_loop_add(3) for the
full reason.

Inside the library, one persistent entry in the epoll interest list
backs each fd that you register. Both directions share one entry,
because epoll_ctl keys its interest list by fd, and not by a (fd,
direction) pair. Each queue selectable or ccol_channel selectable gets
its own dedicated bridge eventfd(2). The library links that eventfd
permanently into the waiter list of the queue. It is not one of the
transient waiter nodes that ccol_select() makes for each call.

The registry of the fds and the registrations is lock-striped. It
holds num_lock_stripes independent (mutex, chmap) pairs. Each pair
guards a subset of the registrations that no other pair holds. Exactly
one stripe handles a real fd for its whole lifetime. The same is true
for the private bridge eventfd of a queue registration or a
ccol_channel registration. No operation therefore needs the lock of
more than one stripe. A value of 1 uses a single lock, which is one
mutex and one chmap, with one more array indirection. A larger value
lets ccol_event_loop_add, ccol_event_loop_remove and
ccol_event_loop_modify calls for different fds or registrations run at
the same time. They then do not serialize through a single lock. The
cost is num_lock_stripes mutexes and chmaps that the library allocates
up front.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
max_events_per_wait  Size of the event batch buffer that the
                      poller thread uses. It must be between 1 and
                      INT_MAX, and both ends count. It must also be
                      small enough that max_events_per_wait times the
                      size of one kernel event record (struct
                      epoll_event on Linux, struct kevent on FreeBSD)
                      does not overflow size_t. The library narrows
                      the value to the int event count parameter of
                      epoll_wait(2) or kevent(2). It also
                      sizes the events buffer of the poller thread
                      from it.
num_lock_stripes     Number of independent lock stripes for the
                      registry of the fds and the entries. It must be
                      >= 1. A value of 1 means a single shared lock.
                      The library applies no upper bound.
num_reactor_threads  Total number of OS threads that this loop uses
                      for its own poll work and dispatch work. It must
                      be >= 1. A value of 1 means a single thread that
                      polls and dispatches inline. Any larger value
                      means exactly one dedicated poller thread, plus
                      (num_reactor_threads - 1) dispatch worker
                      threads.
mmgmt_procs          Custom memory management procedures, or NULL for
                      the default malloc and free.
err_str               Optional pointer that gets an error string on a
                      failure. Pass NULL to ignore it.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a new ccol_event_loop handle on success.
Returns CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_INVALID on a failure. The reasons for a
failure are: max_events_per_wait is 0; max_events_per_wait is larger
than INT_MAX; max_events_per_wait overflows size_t when the library
multiplies it by the size of one kernel event record;
num_lock_stripes is 0;
num_reactor_threads is 0; mmgmt_procs is malformed, because it sets
some but not all of malloc, calloc, realloc and free; an allocation
failed; epoll_create1(2) (kqueue(2) on FreeBSD), eventfd(2) or
pthread_create(3) failed; the
process had no thread-specific key left for the two keys that the
dispatch of every loop uses (the library creates them once for each
process and does not try again); the process-exit destructors of the
library have run and the last loop is gone, for example in an
application destructor that runs after them; or the library could not
allocate a handle slot. In that last case the library stops the poller
thread again before it returns, and the dispatch pool too, if the
configuration made one.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The handle that this function gives is an opaque VALUE, and not a
pointer. Do not cast it to void* or from void*. Do not use it as an
address. Compare it with CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_INVALID. That value is 0. Therefore,
a test such as if (!loop) is also correct. Before the library uses the
loop, it resolves the handle. It therefore refuses a stale handle or a
handle that a call destroyed. It never dereferences such a handle.

This page names the epoll(7) calls and flags of the Linux build. The
FreeBSD build runs the same reactor on kqueue(2), with the same
behaviour: level-triggered reporting, one-shot re-arming when a
dispatch pool exists, and the same delivery of errors and hang-ups.

More than one independent ccol_event_loop instance can live in the
same process. Each one owns its own epoll instance, its own reactor
threads and its own array of lock stripes. It shares no global state
with any other instance. num_lock_stripes and num_reactor_threads are
both settings of one instance, and they are truly separate for each
one.

Exactly one thread ever blocks in epoll_wait, whatever
num_reactor_threads is. If more than one thread called epoll_wait on
the same shared instance, a single ready event could wake every one of
them. That is a thundering herd inside the kernel. EPOLLEXCLUSIVE does
not help here. It governs the same fd that you register across more
than one SEPARATE epoll instance. It does not govern many threads that
share one instance. Such a wake-up wastes dispatch latency on a single
connection that nothing else contends for.

There are two supported ways to combine fork(2) with ccol_event_loop.
The first is to fork(2) before you create a loop. The second is to
create one, then fork(2) and exec(3) at once. A process that keeps
running with no exec(3) in between, while a loop that it created
before the fork is still live, is not a supported pattern.

The library adds one measure of defence in depth on top of that rule.
A fresh call from any thread never hangs on a lock. This covers
ccol_event_loop_create_with_mprocs, _add, _remove, _modify, _pause,
_resume and _destroy. It holds in the parent, and in a child that
something just forked. Such a lock is one that some other thread held
at the instant of the fork. That thread may no longer exist, because
fork(2) duplicates only the thread that calls it.

A loop that is already live across the fork is a different matter, and
that measure does not change it. The child gets no copy of its poller
thread and its dispatch threads. A loop that the child inherits can
therefore no longer dispatch anything there. Treat such a loop as
inert in the child. It is safe to call ccol_event_loop_destroy on it,
and you cannot use it for any more dispatch.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
Safe to call at the same time as work on unrelated ccol_event_loop
instances. A call "on" a handle that does not exist yet has no
meaning.
.fi
